# Flow DSL

The Flow DSL is compile-time Elixir syntax for canonical Flow data. It is not
general Elixir code. Put executable work in Actions.

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install The Package

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Define Small Actions

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.Echo do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_language_echo"

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end

defmodule FlowLanguage.Append do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_language_append"

  @impl true
  def run(%{values: values, item: item}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{values: values ++ [item]}}
  end
end

defmodule FlowLanguage.Increment do
  use Jido.Action, name: "flow_language_increment"

  @impl true
  def run(%{count: count}, _context), do: {:ok, %{count: count + 1}}
end
```

## Declare The Graph

The six component forms are `step`, `choice`, `map`, `reduce`, `iterate`, and
a Flow-derived `subflow`. A child Flow is written as a `step` whose target is a
Flow module.

```elixir
defmodule FlowLanguage.Example do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "flow_language_example"

  flow do
    step "seed",
      action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}

    choice "route" do
      option "fast",
        condition: input(:mode) == :fast,
        action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
        params: %{route: :fast, value: result("seed", :value)}

      otherwise(
        action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
        params: %{route: :standard, value: result("seed", :value)}
      )
    end

    map "mapped",
      collection: input(:items),
      action: FlowLanguage.Echo,
      params: %{value: item(), index: item_index()}

    reduce "collected" do
      collection(result("mapped"))
      initial(%{values: value([])})
      action(FlowLanguage.Append)
      params(%{
        values: accumulator(:values),
        item: item(:value)
      })
    end

    iterate "counter" do
      state(Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer()}), initial: %{count: 0})
      action(FlowLanguage.Increment)
      params(%{count: state(:count)})
      update(%{count: body_result(:count)})
      repeat(2)
    end

    output(%{
      route: result("route"),
      values: result("collected", :values),
      counter: result("counter", :state)
    })
  end
end
```

```elixir
{:ok, result} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowLanguage.Example, %{
    value: 10,
    mode: :fast,
    items: [1, 2, 3]
  })

result
```

## Use Only Data Expressions

A Flow expression can contain:

- `nil`, Boolean values, numbers, strings, and atoms;
- proper lists;
- maps with supported data keys; and
- Flow references.

These forms are not Flow expressions:

```text
function calls
anonymous functions
pipes
pattern matches
tuples
keyword lists
struct construction
```

Use `value/1` when you want to mark one supported literal explicitly. Use
`select/2` to append a path to a reference. The Reduce example uses
`value([])` to mark its empty-list accumulator as literal data.

## Use Closed Conditions

The DSL supports these condition forms:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
input(:status) == :ready
input(:count) >= 3
input(:tier) in [:gold, :silver]
input(:enabled) == true and input(:blocked) != true
not (input(:status) == :closed)
```

The canonical operators are `eq`, `neq`, `lt`, `lte`, `gt`, `gte`, `in`,
`all`, `any`, and `not`.

## Keep Output Explicit

Every Flow has exactly one `output` declaration, and it must be the final
declaration. Normal Flow output must be a map. An explicit
`Jido.Action.Output` envelope can carry a non-map result.

Continue with [Steps And Output](flow-steps.livemd) and
[References And Data](flow-references.livemd).
